NAME
    tls-ca - authorize and revoke extra system-scoped TLS certificate authorities

SYNOPSIS
    tls-ca add <certificate.pem>
    tls-ca remove <sha256-fingerprint>
    tls-ca list

DESCRIPTION
    Administratively manages additional public root CA certificates used by
    explicitly integrated m TLS clients. The authority belongs to system
    sys/tls/conf resolved through state-path. It does not alter the host trust
    store, browser profiles, client certificates or upstream public CA roots.

    add accepts exactly one PEM-encoded public CA certificate, validates CA
    capability and the SHA-256 fingerprint, and publishes a fingerprint-named
    certificate atomically. Duplicate approval of the same certificate is
    idempotent. Private keys and multi-certificate bundles are rejected.

    remove deletes the exact approved fingerprint. list prints fingerprints of
    approved certificates, one per line. Removed trust can persist in running
    browser processes until restart and can remain independently trusted via
    the host or another application-specific truststore.

    The selected system state must be protected by host ownership and
    permissions. Giving an untrusted POSIX account write access to this
    configuration grants that account administrative trust authority.
    tls-ca does not use m user-state bindings as authorization principals.

OPERANDS
    certificate.pem
        Readable PEM certificate containing one public CA certificate.

    sha256-fingerprint
        Exactly 64 lowercase hexadecimal characters, shown by add and list.

DEPENDENCIES
    OpenSSL is required for add. The filesystem account running the command
    must be permitted to modify system sys/tls/conf/ca. Readers require
    traversal/read permission to use the public certificates.

ENVIRONMENT
    Requires a nonempty m_ROOT inherited from an initialized m environment.
    From an uninitialized host shell, invoke m tls-ca [arguments].


EXIT STATUS
    0    Operation completed, including idempotent add and empty list.
    2    Invalid command syntax.
    3-19 Distinct authorization-state, certificate, dependency or filesystem
         failures, diagnosed on standard error with tls-ca.<branch> identity.

EXAMPLES
    tls-ca add /path/to/corporate-root.pem
    tls-ca list
    tls-ca remove <fingerprint-from-add>

SEE ALSO
    tls-nss-sync
    state-path
